Id: no-test-outside-a-tests-file
Pattern: ^[[:space:]]*\[(Fact|Theory)\b
Subject: *Tests/*.cs
Except: :!*Tests/*Tests.cs
Except-Glob: :!*Tests/*Tests.cs
Issue: 49

A test declared in a file the suite's naming convention says is not a test file
is a test no rule written against test files can see. This rule exists so that
`no-server-type-in-a-test` means what its prose says: that rule's subject is
`*Tests/*Tests.cs`, so that the fakes standing in for server interfaces are
outside it by construction rather than exempted by a list that grows with every
fake. A test written into a file named after what it does instead would sit in
the same place as those fakes and be judged by neither.

The convention is the suite's own and was not invented here. A file whose name
ends `Tests.cs` holds tests; every other file in the test project is apparatus -
a fake, a recorder, a seed, a mutation set - and is named after what it is. That
convention was already whole when this rule landed, so nothing was renamed for
it.

What it catches is the honest mistake rather than an evasion: a test added to
the file that already holds the fake it drives, because that is where the
author was already typing. The consequence is invisible at the moment it is
made and it is not the test failing, it is a neighbouring rule going quiet on
one file with nothing red anywhere.

The pattern reads the attribute at the start of a line, which is where an
attribute on a method sits in this tree, and it names the two xunit declares a
test with. `[InlineData]` and `[MemberData]` are deliberately not here: they
decorate a test rather than declare one, and a file carrying them without a
`[Theory]` has no test in it.

THE EXCEPTION IS A GLOB AND IT IS DECLARED AS ONE, WHICH IS THE OTHER ANSWER
#387 ALLOWS. Every other exception in this directory was a filename suffix a
later file could choose, and each of those is now the path it meant. This one
cannot be a path: it has to cover every test file the suite will ever hold,
which is a set nobody can enumerate today, and writing it as a list would mean
editing this rule with every test added.

WHAT STOPS A SECOND FILE TAKING IT is that taking it is not an escape. This
exception is the complement of the subject rather than a hole in it: a file that
renames itself to end `Tests.cs` has renamed itself into being a test file, and
`no-server-type-in-a-test` reads exactly the same pathspec as its subject. So the
name that leaves this rule enters that one, and what a file gains by choosing it
is the rule it was avoiding. That is the difference between this glob and the
eight suffixes #387 refused: those named a seam, which is a place, and a file
that took the name went outside every rule that would have judged it.

Its bounds. An attribute written on the same line as the method it decorates is
not at the start of a line and is not matched. A test declared by a custom
attribute of this repository's own would be a name this pattern does not carry,
and there is none. And a file inside the subject that holds no test at all is
not the thing being refused: what is refused is a test outside a test file, so
apparatus stays where it is and needs nothing.
